Every so often, a tool comes along that's hailed as a revolution in simplicity. Markdown was one of
them. Birthed from a desire to write for the web back in the early 2000s without the fuss of HTML
tags /and give it back to writers/, it promised a clean, readable, and straightforward future. For a
quick forum post or a `README` file on VCS forges such as GitHub, fine. I'll grant it that. But for
anything destined for professional publication, for anything that requires *structure*, *consistency*,
and *semantic integrity*? Frankly, it's a disaster.

Markdown is a solution in search of a problem it /spectacularly/ fails to solve, all while creating a
bigger, more insidious one for anyone who /actually/ cares about the final, published word. It is the
typesetting equivalent of being handed a bucket of LEGOs and being told to build a long-bearing wall.
What follows is not a comprehensive takedonw, but a programmer's rant over a syntax that has duped
the world into believing that “simple” is a synonym for “good”.

** The Grand Illusion: A “Standard” That Isn't
   Let's get one thing straight, because this is where the /whole/ rotten edifice begins to crumble.
   The word “standard” implies a certain... well, /standard/. A bedrock of agreement. A promise
   that what I write here will look the same over there. Markdown's “standard” is *none* of those
   things.

   I'm not a religious person, but if I were, I'd say that Markdown's first sin was the lie that it
   pretended to be a single, definable /thing/. It wasn't. It still isn't. What John Gruber unleashed
   on the world back in 2004 was less a technical specification and more a [charmingly vague blog
   post]{https://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/} with a side of Perl script and lots of ambiguity. It was a philosophy, a suggestion, an
   “I'm sure this will be fine” shrug into the digital void. The problem is, for anything to
   actually work consistently, you need rules. Hard rules. Not just a vibe.

   Becuase the original recipe was missing half the ingredients, everyone who used it had to
   /immediately/ start patching it up. What you think of as “Markdown” isn't Markdown. It's Markdown
   plus a whole lot of digital duct tape. It's a fractured mess of dialects, a fucking Tower of
   Babel in real life built from asterisks and hashtags.

   An entire committee had to be formed—a goddamn committee!—just to try and hammer out a
   baseline called [CommonMark]{https://commonmark.org/}, because the original spec was so damn ambiguous that two different
   parsers could look at the same text and produce entirely /different/ results (see the [CommonMark
   Spec]{https://spec.commonmark.org/}). And that's before we even get to the 800-kilogram gorilla, [Github Flavored Markdown]{https://github.github.com/gfm/} (GFM),
   or the mad scientist's toolkit in Pandoc's version. Each one has its own special flavor of
   bullshit, a delightful topic we'll dissect later on as if we were running a `git bisect` to find
   out which damn commit broke the whole piece of software (spoiler: it was every /single/ commit).

   You think I'm exaggerating? Fine. Try writing this perfectly simple snippet:
   @code markdown
   * Here is a list item.
     * And a nested item.

             With a bit of indented text.
   @end

   How does that render? Is the indented text a code block? Or just a paragraph attached to the
   nested item? The answer, my friend, is a glorious “[mayhaps]{https://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=Mayhaps.}”. It depends entirely on which
   pastor is preaching from which Markdown bible. One parser will give you a neat code block out of
   nowhere, another will give you a plain paragraph (as I think it should), and a third might just
   throw up its hands and break the list entirely. You can also see it as Russian roulette, except
   instead of shooting yourself, you shoot your document. Fantastic, don't you think?

   So the very first promise—that you can write a simple text file and trust it to be the same
   everywhere—is a filthy lie. Your document isn't truly portable. It's a prisoner of whatever
   platform you wrote it on. It’s not a standard. It's a suggestion that got wildly out of hand,
   leaving a trail of broken formatting and shattered expectations in its wake.
